.TH LIBESDM_ES_EXT 3 "2026-10-19" "ESDM @VERSION@" "ESDM Manual"
.SH NAME
libesdm_es_ext \- external entropy sources for the ESDM
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <esdm/esdm_es_ext.h>
.PP
.BI "int esdm_es_ext_provider_main(const struct esdm_es_ext_plugin *" plugin ", int " argc ", char *" argv "[]);"
.PP
.B pkg\-config \-\-cflags \-\-libs esdm_es_ext
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
An external entropy source is noise source code that is not part of the ESDM.
It is served to
.BR esdm\-server (8)
by a provider: a process of its own, which the ESDM reads over a UNIX domain
socket, named with
.B esdm\-server \-\-es_ext_socket
.I PATH
or the meson option
.BR es_ext_socket_path .
The ESDM loads no code of a source into its own process: a provider keeps its
own privileges, its own address space and its own life cycle, at the cost of a
round trip per seed \- a source that crashes takes its provider down, not the
ESDM, which connects again once the provider is back.
.PP
A provider is written against this library: the callbacks of the source, a
.B struct esdm_es_ext_plugin
of the header
.IR esdm_es_ext.h ,
and a
.BR main ()
handing them to
.BR esdm_es_ext_provider_main (),
which takes the command line of a provider program and serves them \- see
.BR "PROVIDER PROGRAMS" .
The functions of the provider server serve them from a program of its own
making instead, and the protocol below lets a provider be written without the
library at all.
.BR esdm\-es\-pkcs11 (8)
is a provider program of this library. A provider may serve the ESDM of
another host as well, on SCTP with DTLS and certificates \- see
.BR NETWORK .
.PP
An ESDM may be built with several provider socket sources (meson option
.BR es_ext_socket_num ,
one by default): each is an entropy source of its own, named
.BR ExternalSocket0 ,
.BR ExternalSocket1 ,
\&... in the status of the ESDM \- or
.B ExternalSocket
as the one source \- with its own provider, entropy rate, accepted user and
state. A provider serves one source, as its callbacks are never called
concurrently; several providers serve on sockets of their own, see
.BR "SEVERAL SOURCES" .
.SS The callbacks of a source
.nf
struct esdm_es_ext_plugin {
	uint32_t version;
	const char *name;
	int (*init)(void **ctx);
	void (*fini)(void *ctx);
	int (*get_entropy)(void *ctx, uint8_t *buf, size_t buflen,
			   uint32_t *entropy_bits);
	int (*selftest)(void *ctx);
	int (*status_json)(void *ctx, char *buf, size_t buflen);
	uint32_t (*entropy_rate)(void *ctx);
};
.fi
.PP
The struct keeps the name it had when a source could be loaded into the ESDM
as a plugin library: providers are compiled against it.
.I version
is
.BR ESDM_ES_EXT_PLUGIN_VERSION ,
1. A later version may add members at the end of the struct; a source of an
earlier one, whose struct ends before them, is then served all the same. The
provider server refuses callbacks of a version it does not know, or without
.IR get_entropy ,
with
.BR \-EPROTO .
.PP
.I name
is the name of the source: the provider declares it in its answer to INFO, and
the ESDM shows the source under it in its status \- the
.B name
member of the source \- and in its log, in place of the name of the entropy
source it reads the provider with
.RB ( ExternalSocket ,
or
.BR ExternalSocket0 ,
.B ExternalSocket1
and so on with several), which the status keeps as the
.B instance
of the source. It is 1 to
.B ESDM_ES_EXT_NAME_MAX
(32) bytes of UTF-8 without control characters \- no C0, DEL or C1 code point,
nor a bidirectional control (U+061C, U+200E, U+200F, U+202A to U+202E, U+2066
to U+2069) \- terminated by a NUL. A name that is none of that, or the name of
another entropy source of the ESDM, is refused, not cut to size, and the
source shown under its instance name, as is a source whose
.I name
is NULL. Two sources may declare the same name; their instances tell them
apart. The provider server takes the name when it sets the source up, after
.IR init .
.I get_entropy
fills all
.I buflen
bytes of
.I buf
and sets
.I *entropy_bits
to the entropy they hold, at most
.IR buflen " * 8,"
returning 0 \- or a negative errno if the source cannot deliver, upon which
the data is discarded. It must not block for long, as a DRNG of the ESDM waits
for the seed it is part of, and the ESDM gives a provider 2 seconds per
request. The other callbacks may be NULL;
.I init
returns the context handed to the others,
.I selftest
checks the source without consuming the data it delivers. It runs before the
ESDM credits the source for the first time \- at start up before the ESDM
seeds its DRNGs \-, on every new connection of the ESDM to the provider \- one
that was not there at start up, or one restarted \- before the provider is
credited, and on the interval of the ESDM self tests.
.PP
.I status_json
writes the status of the source \- whatever helps whoever looks at the ESDM
status, such as the device read or the health of the noise source \- into
.I buf
as one JSON object terminated by a NUL, at most
.B ESDM_ES_EXT_STATUS_MAX
= 512 bytes of text, for which
.I buflen
leaves room. It returns 0,
.B \-EMSGSIZE
if the status does not fit, or another negative errno. The provider server
answers STATUS with it as the
.B plugin
member of its own status, and the ESDM shows that as the
.B provider
member of the state of the source, in
.BR esdm_status_json ()
and as a section in the text of
.BR esdm_status ().
A status that is no well-formed JSON object of up to 512 bytes and 8 levels of
nesting, without NaN or infinities, and without a C1 control or a
bidirectional control in a string or a member name, escaped or not, is left
out: these would steer the terminal of whoever reads the status. It is asked
for when the ESDM status is, at most every 5 seconds \- the last status
obtained shown in between \- and not while a request of the ESDM to the source
is in progress, the ESDM status going without it then. It must not block for
long: the source waits for it, and so does a seeding.
.PP
The callbacks are never called concurrently: the provider server calls them
one at a time, from one thread. They all run in the process of the provider,
.I init
with the privileges the provider was started with \- a device the source
needs is to be opened there: a provider program serving on the network, started
as root, drops its privileges right afterwards
.RB ( \-\-username ).
.SS Entropy credit
The ESDM credits a block with the entropy the source claims for it, but at
most with what the entropy rate of the source allows: the rate configured for
the source
.RB ( es_ext_socket_entropy_rate ,
.BR "esdm\-server \-\-es_ext_socket_entropy_rate" ,
or
.BR esdm_config_es_ext_socket_entropy_rate_set ()
of libesdm), else the rate the provider declares, else 0, so that a provider
declaring none is credited only once a rate is configured. The status of the
source shows the rate as
.B entropy_rate
and where it comes from as
.B entropy_rate_source
.RB ( config ,
.B provider
or
.BR default ).
.PP
A source declares its rate with
.IR entropy_rate ,
bits of entropy per 256 bits of data from 0 to
.B ESDM_ES_EXT_ENTROPY_RATE_MAX
(256); a rate beyond is taken as 256. It is called once after
.IR init ,
so that the source can decide from the hardware it found. The provider
declares the rate in its answer to INFO, see below; the provider server of
this library declares the one of
.IR entropy_rate ,
or the one set with
.BR esdm_es_ext_server_set_entropy_rate ()
\- the
.B \-\-entropy_rate
of a provider program. A provider is another process, trusted for the user it
runs as: accepting that user
.RB ( "esdm\-server \-\-es_ext_socket_uid" )
means trusting the rate the provider declares as well. An operator who does
not want that configures the rate of the source, which takes precedence. A
claim beyond the size of the block is a failure: the block is discarded, and
the source is credited with nothing until it delivers properly again \- the
ESDM asks a failed source again on its own to find out. A source is credited
with nothing before its first
.IR selftest ,
and a source failing it with nothing until a self test passes.
.SH FUNCTIONS
.SS Provider program
.TP
.BI "int esdm_es_ext_provider_main(const struct esdm_es_ext_plugin *" plugin ", int " argc ", char *" argv "[]);"
The
.BR main ()
of a provider program: take the command line of
.I argc
and
.I argv
\- see
.BR "PROVIDER PROGRAMS" ,
.I argv[0]
naming the program in its usage \-, set the source up \- call the
.I init
callback of
.I plugin
\- and serve it, on a UNIX domain socket or, in a library built with the meson
option
.BR es\-ext\-net ,
on the network, until the program is ended by a signal. Returns the exit
status of the program: 0 once a signal ended it, or for
.B \-\-help
and
.BR \-\-version ;
1 for a command line that is wrong, a source that cannot be set up or served
\- told on stderr \- or serving that failed. A program calls it once, from its
.BR main (),
and returns what it returns: it parses the command line with
.BR getopt_long (3),
serves one source per process, and logs to stderr.
.SS Provider server
.TP
.BI "int esdm_es_ext_server_alloc(const struct esdm_es_ext_plugin *" plugin ", const char *" socket_path ", mode_t " mode ", struct esdm_es_ext_server **" server );
Create the
.B SOCK_SEQPACKET
socket file
.I socket_path
with exactly the access mode
.I mode
\- whatever the umask \- and call the
.I init
callback of
.IR plugin .
A socket file left at the path by an earlier instance is replaced, any other
file is not
.RB ( \-EEXIST ).
The ESDM connects with its privileges dropped (user
.B nobody
by default), so the mode has to let that user connect. Returns
.B \-EPROTO
for callbacks that do not fit this version of the API, and the errno
.I init
returns if it fails, leaving no socket behind.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_es_ext_server_allow_uid(struct esdm_es_ext_server *" server ", uid_t " uid );
Serve only clients running as root or as an allowed user, up to 8 of them;
any other client is disconnected before it can take one of the few client
slots of the server, or drain the noise source. Without a user allowed, any
client that can connect is served. Allow the user the ESDM runs as: it
connects as root at start up, but with its privileges dropped once it
reconnects.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_es_ext_server_set_name(struct esdm_es_ext_server *" server ", const char *" name );
Declare
.I name
as the name of the source in the answer to INFO, in place of the
.I name
of the source, which the server declares by default \- if it is a valid one.
Returns
.B \-EINVAL
for a name that is not valid; NULL goes back to the name of the source. Call
it before
.BR esdm_es_ext_server_run ().
.TP
.BI "int esdm_es_ext_server_set_entropy_rate(struct esdm_es_ext_server *" server ", uint32_t " rate );
Declare
.I rate
bits of entropy per 256 bits of data, 0 to 256, as the rate of the source in
the answer to INFO, in place of the one the
.I entropy_rate
callback of the source returns, which the server declares by default \- none
for a source without it. Returns
.B \-EINVAL
for a rate beyond 256;
.B ESDM_ES_EXT_PROTO_RATE_NONE
goes back to the rate of the source. Call it before
.BR esdm_es_ext_server_run ().
.TP
.BI "int esdm_es_ext_server_set_dtls(struct esdm_es_ext_server *" server ", int " enable );
Offer DTLS to the clients, or not. A library built with DTLS offers it by
default; enabling it in one built without returns
.BR \-EOPNOTSUPP .
.TP
.BI "int esdm_es_ext_server_run(struct esdm_es_ext_server *" server );
Serve the connected clients, one request after the other, until
.BR esdm_es_ext_server_stop ()
is called. A client that breaks the protocol is disconnected. Up to 8 clients
are served at a time: when another one connects, the client that sent nothing
for the longest time is disconnected to make room for it \- one running as
root only for another one running as root, as the ESDM connects as root at
start up. Clients holding their slots without using them so cannot lock the
ESDM out, which connects again at once when it finds its connection closed.
.TP
.BI "void esdm_es_ext_server_stop(struct esdm_es_ext_server *" server );
Make
.BR esdm_es_ext_server_run ()
return. Callable from another thread and from a signal handler.
.TP
.BI "void esdm_es_ext_server_free(struct esdm_es_ext_server *" server );
Close the connections, remove the socket file \- the one created, not one
another instance put there meanwhile \- and call the
.I fini
callback of the source.
.SS Provider server on the network
A library built with the meson option
.B es\-ext\-net
serves a source to the ESDM of other hosts as well, on SCTP with DTLS and
certificates \- see
.BR NETWORK .
The other functions of the provider server apply to such a server as well,
but
.BR esdm_es_ext_server_allow_uid (),
which returns
.B \-EINVAL
\- a client is known by its certificate \- and
.BR esdm_es_ext_server_set_dtls (),
which cannot switch the DTLS of the network off.
.TP
.BI "void esdm_es_ext_server_net_config_init(struct esdm_es_ext_server_net_config *" cfg );
Set
.I cfg
to the defaults: no listeners and no TLS files, the TLS files checked for a
change every 60 seconds, every client address allowed, up to 64 connections in
all, 16 in their handshake and 4 of them per source address \- per /64 for
IPv6 \-, 4 per client certificate, 10 seconds for a handshake and 600 seconds
of silence. The struct carries its
.IR size :
fields are only ever appended, and the library takes the defaults for those a
caller built against an older header does not know.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_es_ext_server_alloc_net(const struct esdm_es_ext_plugin *" plugin ", const struct esdm_es_ext_server_net_config *" cfg ", struct esdm_es_ext_server **" server );
As
.BR esdm_es_ext_server_alloc (),
on the SCTP sockets of
.IR cfg :
the addresses of
.I listen
\- "HOST:PORT", "[IPv6]:PORT", ":PORT" \- and the listening sockets of
.I listen_fds
handed over, SCTP of the one-to-one style. Loads the certificate, key, trust
anchors and CRLs of
.IR cfg ,
binds the listeners and calls the
.I init
callback of the source: a daemon drops its privileges afterwards. Returns
.B \-EINVAL
for a configuration that does not parse or TLS files that do not load \- both
logged \-,
.B \-EPROTONOSUPPORT
without SCTP in the kernel, and
.B \-EOPNOTSUPP
in a library built without
.BR es\-ext\-net .
.TP
.BI "int esdm_es_ext_server_reload(struct esdm_es_ext_server *" server );
Have
.BR esdm_es_ext_server_run ()
take up the TLS files at once \- when they load completely, else the ones
loaded before stay in service \- and close the connections of the clients the
new CRLs revoke. Callable from another thread and from a signal handler.
Returns
.B \-EINVAL
for a server on a UNIX domain socket.
.SH RETURN VALUE
The functions of the provider server returning an int return 0 on success and
a negative errno on failure;
.BR esdm_es_ext_provider_main ()
returns the exit status of the program.
.SH PROVIDER PROGRAMS
A program whose
.BR main ()
calls
.BR esdm_es_ext_provider_main ()
\- or
.BR esdm\-es\-pkcs11 (8),
which takes the options of its token next to these \- serves its source on
the UNIX domain socket
.I PATH
of
.BR \-\-socket ,
which
.B esdm\-server \-\-es_ext_socket
.I PATH
connects to:
.PP
.nf
my\-provider \-\-socket \fIPATH\fR [\-\-mode \fIMODE\fR] [\-\-client_uid \fIUSER\fR]...
	[\-\-name \fINAME\fR] [\-\-entropy_rate \fIBITS\fR] [\-v]
my\-provider \-\-listen [\fIHOST\fR]:\fIPORT\fR [\-\-cert \fIFILE\fR] [\-\-key \fIFILE\fR]
	[\-\-ca \fIFILE\fR] [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.fi
.PP
With
.B \-\-listen
or sockets handed over by socket activation, it serves the source to the ESDM
of other hosts instead \- on SCTP, with DTLS and certificates, see
.BR NETWORK .
.PP
The source is set up \- its
.I init
called \- before the socket is served; callbacks that do not fit, or an
.I init
that fails, end the program with exit status 1, as does a command line that
is wrong. It serves until it receives
.BR SIGTERM ,
.B SIGINT
or \- on a UNIX domain socket \-
.BR SIGHUP ,
then removes the socket, calls the
.I fini
callback and exits with status 0. On the network,
.B SIGHUP
reloads the TLS files. The signals are blocked in every thread from the start
\- one that
.I init
starts included \- and taken by a thread of their own. Once it serves, the
program tells systemd so where it runs as a service of
.B Type=notify
\- in a library built with the meson option
.BR systemd .
.PP
The ESDM accepts a provider running as root, or as the user given with
.BR "esdm\-server \-\-es_ext_socket_uid" .
It connects with its privileges dropped once a connection was lost, so the
socket has to let the user the ESDM runs as connect \- by default
.BR nobody ,
hence the default mode 0666. A more restrictive mode needs the ESDM to run with
a group that is granted access
.RB ( "esdm\-server \-\-groupname" ).
.PP
The
.I name
of the source \- or the one given with
.B \-\-name
\- is the name the ESDM shows the source under, which it asks the provider for
on every connection (INFO request, see
.BR PROTOCOL ).
The rate the
.I entropy_rate
callback of the source declares \- or the one given with
.B \-\-entropy_rate
\- is the entropy rate the ESDM credits the provider at, unless a rate is
configured for the source in the ESDM
.RB ( "esdm\-server \-\-es_ext_socket_entropy_rate" ),
which takes precedence. Without either, the provider declares none and the
ESDM credits it at 0 unless configured otherwise. Note that whoever runs a
provider of a user the ESDM accepts can declare any rate this way: the ESDM
operator who does not want to trust that configures the rate of the source.
The rate the source declares is logged at start up.
.PP
In turn, on a UNIX domain socket, only clients running as root or as a user
given with
.B \-\-client_uid
are served \- by default root and
.BR nobody ,
the user the ESDM runs as unless told otherwise. Any other client is
disconnected at once, so that it can neither take the few client slots the
ESDM needs nor drain the noise source. Up to 8 clients are served at a time,
see
.BR esdm_es_ext_server_run ().
.SS Options
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-socket " \fIPATH\fR"
Socket to serve on. A socket left at the path by an earlier instance is
replaced, any other file is not. Required, but on the network \- a program
with a socket of its own, as
.BR esdm\-es\-pkcs11 (8),
serves that one without it.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-mode " \fIMODE\fR"
Access mode of the socket, octal. The default is 0666.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-client_uid " \fIUSER\fR"
Serve clients running as
.I USER
\- a uid or a user name \- next to root. May be given up to 8 times; the
default is
.BR nobody ,
or root alone without a user of that name. Give the user of
.B esdm\-server \-\-username
if the ESDM runs as another one.
.TP
.BI \-\-name " NAME"
Declare
.I NAME
as the name of the source in place of its own: 1 to 32 bytes of UTF-8 without
control characters. A name that is none of that is refused at start up, with
exit status 1.
.TP
.BI \-\-entropy_rate " BITS"
Declare
.I BITS
of entropy per 256 bits of data, 0 to 256, as the rate of the source in place
of the one it declares itself. Anything else is refused at start up, with exit
status 1.
.TP
.B \-\-no_dtls
Do not offer DTLS to the clients. A library built with DTLS (meson option
.BR dtls )
offers it by default, see
.BR DTLS .
.PP
.BR \-\-socket ,
.BR \-\-mode ,
.B \-\-client_uid
and
.B \-\-no_dtls
are for a UNIX domain socket, and refused on the network. The options below
are for the network, and refused without
.B \-\-listen
or sockets handed over \- in a library built with the meson option
.BR es\-ext\-net :
.TP
.BI \-\-listen " [HOST]:PORT"
Listen on
.I PORT
of
.I HOST
\- an address or a name; an IPv6 address in brackets, as in
.BR [2001:db8::1]:@ES_EXT_NET_PORT@ .
Without
.IR HOST ,
or with
.BR * ,
on every address. May be given up to 16 times. Sockets handed over by socket
activation
.RB ( LISTEN_FDS )
\- SCTP of the one-to-one style \- are served next to these. The relays
connect to port @ES_EXT_NET_PORT@ by default (meson option
.BR es\-ext\-net\-port ).
.TP
.BI \-\-cert " FILE"
Certificate of the provider, PEM, optionally followed by the intermediate
certificates of its chain, issued for the name the relays connect to. It has
to chain to the trust anchors of
.B \-\-ca
as well: the provider and its clients are issued in one trust domain.
.TP
.BI \-\-key " FILE"
Its private key, PEM, unencrypted, PKCS#8.
.TP
.BI \-\-ca " FILE"
Trust anchors the certificates of the clients have to chain to, PEM. A chain
ends in a self\-signed one, see
.B \-\-ca
in
.BR esdm\-egd\-net\-server (8).
.TP
.BI \-\-crl " FILE"
CRLs, PEM; a file may hold several. May be given up to 16 times. Complete CRLs
only, no delta CRLs nor ones with an issuing distribution point. The checks are
those of
.B REVOCATION
in
.BR esdm\-egd\-net\-server (8),
and so are the alerts a refused client is told \- but by a provider of the
Botan backend, whose DTLS 1.2 server tells every client whose certificate it
refuses bad_certificate. Its log tells why.
.TP
.B \-\-crl_check_all
Require a CRL for every CA certificate of a client's chain as well, not only
for its own certificate.
.TP
.B \-\-crl_allow_expired
Use a CRL past its nextUpdate with a warning, rather than refusing the clients
it covers.
.TP
.BI \-\-reload_interval " SEC"
Check the TLS files for a change this often, and take them up when they
changed. 0 checks them on
.B SIGHUP
only. The default is 60.
.TP
.BI \-\-allow " CIDR"
Serve only clients from the network
.IR CIDR ,
such as
.B 192.0.2.0/24
or
.BR 2001:db8::/48 ,
or from a single address. May be given up to 64 times. An association from
anywhere else is closed right after it was accepted. The default is every
address.
.TP
.BI \-\-max_connections " N"
Connections served at a time, in all. The default is 64.
.TP
.BI \-\-max_handshakes " N"
Connections in their handshake at a time. The default is 16.
.TP
.BI \-\-max_handshakes_per_source " N"
The same per source address \- per /64 for IPv6. The default is 4.
.TP
.BI \-\-max_per_client " N"
Connections per client certificate; one beyond is closed right after its
handshake. The default is 4.
.TP
.BI \-\-handshake_timeout " SEC"
A connection that did not complete its handshake by then is closed. The
default is 10.
.TP
.BI \-\-idle_timeout " SEC"
A connection whose client sent no request for this long is closed; 0 for
never. The default is 600. The relay connects again with the next request of
the ESDM.
.TP
.BI \-\-username " USER"
User to switch to after the sockets are bound, the TLS files loaded and the
source set up, when started as root. The default is
.BR nobody .
The files are read as that user when they are taken up later.
.TP
.BI \-\-groupname " GROUP"
Group to switch to; it and the group of the user are the supplementary groups
then. The default is none.
.PP
And on either:
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Verbose logging; repeat for more. On the network, warnings and errors are
logged without it, as by the other daemons on the network.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the name the program was started under and the version of the library.
.SS Status
A provider program answers the STATUS request of the ESDM, which shows the
answer as the
.B provider
member of the source in its own status, with one JSON object of up to 1024
bytes:
.PP
.nf
	{ "server": { ... }, "plugin": ... }
.fi
.PP
.B plugin
is the status the source reports of itself with its
.I status_json
callback \- or null for a source without one, one failing, one whose status
is no JSON object of up to 512 bytes, nested no deeper than 8 levels, without
NaN or infinities and without a C1 control or a bidirectional control in a
string \- see above \- and one that does not fit next to the fields of the
server; never a status cut short.
.B server
holds the fields of the provider server itself:
.TP
.B version
the version of
.BR libesdm_es_ext ;
.TP
.B plugin_api
the
.I version
of the struct esdm_es_ext_plugin of the source;
.TP
.BR name ", " entropy_rate
the name and the entropy rate it declares in its answer to INFO, those of
.B \-\-name
and
.B \-\-entropy_rate
or of the source, null for none;
.TP
.B transport
.B unix
on a UNIX domain socket,
.B sctp
on the network;
.TP
.B dtls
whether the connection asking runs DTLS \- always on the network;
.TP
.B peer
on the network only: the SHA-256 of the certificate of the client asking, in
lower case hex \- that of the
.BR esdm\-es\-ext\-relay (8)
the ESDM reads the provider through;
.TP
.BR clients ", " client_slots
the clients connected, and how many it serves at a time;
.TP
.B requests
the requests it served, of all clients;
.TP
.B selftest
the verdict of the last self test of the source a client asked for:
.BR passed ,
.BR failed ,
or
.B none
before the first;
.TP
.B uptime_seconds
the time since it was started.
.SH NETWORK
A provider program serving on the network is read by the ESDM of another host
through the
.BR esdm\-es\-ext\-relay (8)
of that host, which serves the ESDM a local provider socket and forwards its
requests:
.PP
.nf
	esdm\-server \-\-\-unix socket\-\-\-> esdm\-es\-ext\-relay
		\-\-\-SCTP, DTLS 1.2, certificates\-\-\-> provider program
.fi
.PP
The transport is SCTP, one-to-one style, over IPv4 or IPv6, with DTLS 1.2 as
RFC 6083 maps it onto SCTP:
.IP \(bu 2
Every DTLS record is one SCTP message, on stream 0, ordered; the requests and
answers of the protocol below travel inside the DTLS from the first message on
\- there is no STARTDTLS, and no message in the clear. SCTP retransmits, the
DTLS does not.
.IP \(bu 2
Both sides authenticate with a certificate: the provider checks the client's
against the trust anchors of
.B \-\-ca
and the CRLs of
.BR \-\-crl ,
the relay checks the provider's, and that it is issued for the name it
connects to. A client is known by the SHA-256 of its certificate, which the
STATUS of the provider tells as
.BR peer .
The cipher suites are ECDHE with ECDSA or RSA certificates, and AES-GCM or
ChaCha20-Poly1305. DTLS 1.2 has no post-quantum algorithms: the key exchange
of ML-KEM and the signatures of ML-DSA are defined for TLS 1.3 only, and none
of the TLS stacks has DTLS 1.3. A certificate on ML-DSA is refused at the
start; the certificates may still be issued by a CA on ML-DSA-87, where the
TLS stack verifies ML-DSA \- OpenSSL 3.5 and later, GnuTLS built with
leancrypto, Botan 3.6 and later -, not by one on ML-DSA-44 or ML-DSA-65.
.IP \(bu 2
The association authenticates its DATA and FORWARD-TSN chunks with SCTP-AUTH
(RFC 4895): both sides require it before the association is set up, and refuse
a peer that does not. Each side offers HMAC-SHA-256 first, and HMAC-SHA-1
after it, as RFC 4895 has every endpoint offer it \- Linux takes no list
without. Linux authenticates the chunks it sends with the first HMAC of the
peer's list it supports, and takes either of its own list from the peer:
between the daemons of the ESDM, both directions use HMAC-SHA-256, while a
peer offering HMAC-SHA-1 first is sent HMAC-SHA-1. Linux does not tell which
HMAC an association uses, so the daemons cannot check it. The handshake runs
under key 0, the empty key. Before it sends its ChangeCipherSpec, each side
waits until the peer acknowledged everything it sent, and installs key 1: the
64 bytes of the TLS exporter
.B EXPORTER_DTLS_OVER_SCTP
of the DTLS session, without a context. It makes key 1 the active one once its
ChangeCipherSpec is sent \- the server \- or its Finished \- the client \- and
deletes key 0 once the handshake is complete. Neither side renegotiates, so the
key is set once per association. This is the key handling of RFC 6083 as
draft\-ietf\-tsvwg\-dtls\-over\-sctp\-bis details it for DTLS 1.2, and the one
of OpenSSL's own DTLS over SCTP.
.IP \(bu 2
The kernel needs SCTP (module
.BR sctp ),
and SCTP-AUTH enabled on the sockets, which the daemons do themselves since
Linux 5.4. An older kernel needs the sysctl
.BR net.sctp.auth_enable=1 ;
without it, the provider refuses to start, with an error naming the sysctl. A
firewall in between has to pass SCTP to the port, which a rule for TCP does
not.
.PP
The listener faces the network, and anybody who can reach it may connect, so
the connections are bounded \- a connection is closed at once when it would
exceed a bound: in all
.RB ( \-\-max_connections ),
in their handshake
.RB ( \-\-max_handshakes ,
.BR \-\-max_handshakes_per_source ),
for at most
.BR \-\-handshake_timeout ,
per client
.RB ( \-\-max_per_client ),
and silent for at most
.BR \-\-idle_timeout .
The networks of
.B \-\-allow
limit where the clients may come from.
.PP
Renewed TLS files are taken up without a restart: on
.BR SIGHUP ,
and when one of them changes, which the provider checks every
.B \-\-reload_interval
seconds. A reload builds a complete new context first \- the certificate and
the key parse, the key belongs to the certificate, the certificate chains to
the trust anchors, every CRL parses \- and only then replaces the context in
use; otherwise the error is logged and the old context stays in service, so
that a broken rotation never takes the service down. New handshakes use the
new context, established connections keep theirs. After every reload, the
provider checks the clients of its connections against the new CRLs and closes
those of a revoked certificate; every 30 seconds, those whose certificate
expired.
.PP
A file named without a slash is a credential of the service: it is taken from
.BR $CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY ,
where systemd places what
.B LoadCredential=
names. Without
.BR \-\-cert ,
.B \-\-key
and
.BR \-\-ca ,
the credentials
.BR cert ,
.B key
and
.B ca
are used \- what the units of
.B SYSTEMD
load. The credentials are copies made when the service starts: a rotation
without a restart needs the paths of the files, readable by the user of the
service.
.PP
Whoever holds a client certificate the provider accepts draws on its noise
source; the ESDM behind the relay credits the rate the provider declares \-
see
.B TRUST
in
.BR esdm\-es\-ext\-relay (8).
.SH SYSTEMD
A provider program on a UNIX domain socket is best a service of
.B Type=notify
ordered
.B Before=esdm\-server.service
\- as the unit of
.BR esdm\-es\-pkcs11 (8)
is \-, so that the ESDM finds it at its initial seeding.
.PP
The ESDM ships no units for a provider on the network: such a provider is a
program of the site's making. A socket unit and a service unit serve it on
SCTP port @ES_EXT_NET_PORT@ of every address, as a user of its own
.RB ( DynamicUser= ),
with the TLS files as credentials from
.IR /etc/esdm/es\-ext\-net/ :
.PP
.nf
# /etc/systemd/system/my\-provider\-net.socket
[Unit]
Description=My external entropy source on the network, socket

[Socket]
ListenStream=@ES_EXT_NET_PORT@
SocketProtocol=sctp
Backlog=16

[Install]
WantedBy=sockets.target

# /etc/systemd/system/my\-provider\-net.service
[Unit]
Description=My external entropy source on the network
Requires=my\-provider\-net.socket
After=my\-provider\-net.socket

[Service]
Type=notify
ExecStart=/usr/libexec/my\-provider \-\-allow 192.0.2.0/24
ExecReload=/bin/kill \-HUP $MAINPID
LoadCredential=cert:/etc/esdm/es\-ext\-net/server.crt
LoadCredential=key:/etc/esdm/es\-ext\-net/server.key
LoadCredential=ca:/etc/esdm/es\-ext\-net/ca.crt
DynamicUser=yes
Restart=on\-failure
PrivateDevices=yes
ProtectSystem=strict
NoNewPrivileges=yes
RestrictAddressFamilies=AF_UNIX AF_INET AF_INET6

[Install]
WantedBy=multi\-user.target
Also=my\-provider\-net.socket
.fi
.PP
Enable the service rather than the socket alone:
.PP
.nf
systemctl enable \-\-now my\-provider\-net.service
.fi
.PP
The provider sets the socket up for SCTP-AUTH as it starts, and refuses the
associations the kernel set up before \- a start by the first connection
refuses that one, which the relay makes again on its own. A source reaching a
device needs it granted
.RB ( PrivateDevices=no ,
.BR DeviceAllow= ,
and the group of the device with
.BR SupplementaryGroups= ).
A library built without the meson option
.B systemd
tells systemd nothing: use
.B Type=exec
then. Bind the socket to an address of its own with
.BI ListenStream= ADDRESS : PORT ,
and pass SCTP to the port in the firewall.
.SH SEVERAL SOURCES
A provider program serves one source. An ESDM built with several provider
socket sources (meson option
.BR es_ext_socket_num )
reads several providers, one per source: run one provider per source, each on
a socket of its own, and give the ESDM one
.B \-\-es_ext_socket
per socket, the n-th for its n-th source \- see
.BR esdm\-server (8).
The processes are independent of each other: each is restarted, and fails, on
its own, and the ESDM credits each of them at the rate configured for its
source.
.SH PROTOCOL
The ESDM sends requests on its connection to the provider one at a time; the
provider answers each with exactly one response. Requests and responses are
one
.B SOCK_SEQPACKET
message each, all integers little endian.
.PP
A request is 12 bytes: the magic
.B ESDM_ES_EXT_PROTO_MAGIC
(the bytes "ESEX"), the version
.B ESDM_ES_EXT_PROTO_VERSION
(u16, 1), the type (u16:
.B ESDM_ES_EXT_PROTO_GET_ENTROPY
= 1,
.B ESDM_ES_EXT_PROTO_SELFTEST
= 2,
.B ESDM_ES_EXT_PROTO_STATUS
= 4 or
.B ESDM_ES_EXT_PROTO_INFO
= 5) and the length (u32: the bytes asked for, 1 to
.B ESDM_ES_EXT_PROTO_MAX_DATA
= 512, for GET_ENTROPY; the largest status taken, 1 to
.B ESDM_ES_EXT_PROTO_STATUS_MAX
= 1024, for STATUS; 0 for SELFTEST and INFO).
.PP
A response is 16 bytes: magic, version and type as in the request, the status
(s32: 0 or a negative errno) and the entropy of the data (u32, at most 8 times
the length for GET_ENTROPY, 0 otherwise); a GET_ENTROPY answered with status 0
is followed by exactly the length bytes of data, a STATUS answered with status
0 by the status of the provider \- JSON text of 1 to length bytes without a
NUL \- an INFO answered with status 0 by what the provider declares of itself,
see below, and any other response by nothing.
.PP
A message that breaks any of this ends the connection, and so does a request
of a type the provider does not know.
.SS STATUS
The ESDM asks the provider for its status when its own status is asked for,
at most every 5 seconds, and not while another request is in progress. The
status is one JSON object of up to the length asked for, at most
.B ESDM_ES_EXT_PROTO_STATUS_MAX
(1024) bytes, nested no deeper than 9 levels; the ESDM checks that it is a
well-formed JSON object, without NaN or infinities and without a C1 control
or a bidirectional control in a string \- as the provider server checks the
status of its source, see above \- before it shows it as the
.B provider
member of the source. A provider with no status answers
.BR \-EOPNOTSUPP ,
one whose status does not fit into the length
.BR \-EMSGSIZE .
.PP
The provider server of this library answers with an object of its own,
.PP
.nf
	{ "server": { ... }, "plugin": ... }
.fi
.PP
.B plugin
being the status the
.I status_json
callback of its source reports \- asked for into a buffer of
.B ESDM_ES_EXT_STATUS_MAX
(512) bytes and the NUL \- or null for none, a failing one, one that is no
such object, and one that does not fit into the length next to the fields of
the server, which are never cut short:
.BR version ,
.BR plugin_api ,
the
.B name
and the
.B entropy_rate
it declares in its answer to INFO, its
.B transport
\-
.B unix
or
.B sctp
\-,
.B dtls
of the connection asking and, on the network, the
.B peer
asking, the
.B clients
connected and the
.B client_slots
there are, the
.B requests
served, the
.B selftest
verdict of the source and the
.B uptime_seconds
\- see
.B Status
under
.BR "PROVIDER PROGRAMS" .
So a STATUS of a provider of this library is answered whether its source has a
status or not. A provider written without the library answers with an object
of its own making, within the same bounds.
.PP
The answer travels as one message \- with DTLS, as one record, as it is far
smaller than the part a record carries. A provider older than STATUS closes
the connection on it, as on any request it does not know: the next request of
the ESDM connects again at once, the provider still credited, and the ESDM
does not ask for its status on that connection. A connection found closed
when STATUS is sent, as one to a provider restarted meanwhile, is no such
refusal: the provider is asked on the connection the next request makes. The
ESDM status connects to no provider itself.
.PP
The ESDM status waits for the answer a quarter of the timeout of a request \-
half a second \- at the most, as the source is held meanwhile, and a seeding
waits for it. A provider that answers later keeps its connection: it answers
one request after the other, so its answer comes in before the one to the next
request, and the ESDM takes it off the connection and shows it then \- with
the next request, before it, or with the next ESDM status. Such a provider is
not asked for its status for a minute.
.SS INFO
The ESDM asks the provider what it declares of itself once per connection,
before any other request but STARTDTLS: inside the DTLS session where there is
one. The request has length 0. A provider answers it with status 0, entropy 0
and the data
.PP
.nf
	u32 entropy_rate	0 to 256, or 0xffffffff for none
	u8  name[]		0 to 32 bytes
.fi
.PP
\- 4 to 36 bytes \- where
.I entropy_rate
is the rate the provider declares for its data, bits of entropy per 256 bits,
.B ESDM_ES_EXT_PROTO_RATE_NONE
(0xffffffff) for none, and
.I name
is the name of the source without a NUL, no bytes for a provider without a
name; or with a negative errno, such as
.BR \-EOPNOTSUPP ,
and no data, which declares nothing. The ESDM shows the source under the name,
and takes it only if it is valid as
.I name
of the callbacks of a source has it; a name that is not valid is not taken,
and the source shown under its instance name. It credits the provider at the
rate unless a rate is configured for the source, taking a rate beyond 256 as
256. An answer of more than 36 bytes, or another answer that breaks the
protocol, ends the connection as for any request. The provider server of this
library answers with the
.I name
and the
.I entropy_rate
of its source, or the ones set with
.BR esdm_es_ext_server_set_name ()
and
.BR esdm_es_ext_server_set_entropy_rate ()
\- those of
.B \-\-name
and
.B \-\-entropy_rate
of a provider program. A provider older than INFO closes the connection on it,
as on any request it does not know: the ESDM connects again at once without
asking on the new connection, and uses the provider without a name and a rate.
Both are asked for anew on every connection \- a provider restarted may
declare others.
.SS DTLS
An ESDM built with DTLS (meson option
.BR dtls ,
with the TLS stack of its crypto backend) sends
.B ESDM_ES_EXT_PROTO_STARTDTLS
(type 3, length 0) as its first request. A provider answering it with status 0
expects a DTLS 1.2 handshake on the connection next, the ESDM being the client.
Every DTLS record is one message of the socket; a request or response travels
as DTLS application data, in records carrying one byte in front of their part
of it \- 1 if more parts follow, 0 for the last one. A provider that cannot do
DTLS answers
.BR \-EOPNOTSUPP ,
and the connection goes on unencrypted; one that does not know STARTDTLS closes
the connection, and the ESDM connects again without asking.
.PP
The DTLS is opportunistic and unauthenticated: an ephemeral ECDH key exchange
keyed with a pre\-shared key that is a constant of the ESDM and no secret,
without certificates \- the cipher suites are those of
.BR esdm\-server (8).
What the ESDM trusts the provider for remains the credentials of the process
on the other end of the socket; the DTLS keeps the data from being read off
the channel.
.SS Network
A provider on another host is read through the
.BR esdm\-es\-ext\-relay (8)
of the ESDM's host, which forwards the requests of the ESDM one to one. On
the network, the messages travel over SCTP of the one-to-one style, with DTLS
1.2 as RFC 6083 maps it onto SCTP: every DTLS record is one SCTP message on
stream 0, and every message is DTLS from the first one on \- no STARTDTLS, no
message in the clear, and a STARTDTLS inside the DTLS is a request that breaks
the protocol. Inside, the requests and responses are the ones above, carried
by records with the byte in front. Both sides authenticate with a certificate,
and the relay checks the name of the provider. The association authenticates
its DATA and FORWARD-TSN chunks with SCTP-AUTH (RFC 4895), the key of which is
exported from the DTLS session \- see
.BR NETWORK .
The STATUS of the server tells
.B sctp
as its
.BR transport ,
and as its
.B peer
the SHA-256 of the certificate of the client asking.
.SH EXAMPLE
A provider program: the callbacks of the source, and a
.BR main ()
serving them.
.PP
.nf
#include <errno.h>
#include <esdm/esdm_es_ext.h>

static int my_get_entropy(void *ctx, uint8_t *buf, size_t buflen,
			  uint32_t *entropy_bits)
{
	(void)ctx;

	if (read_my_noise_source(buf, buflen))
		return \-EIO;

	/* The entropy assessment of the noise source: 1 bit per byte */
	*entropy_bits = buflen;
	return 0;
}

static const struct esdm_es_ext_plugin my_source = {
	.version = ESDM_ES_EXT_PLUGIN_VERSION,
	.name = "my\-noise\-source",
	.get_entropy = my_get_entropy,
};

int main(int argc, char *argv[])
{
	return esdm_es_ext_provider_main(&my_source, argc, argv);
}
.fi
.PP
Built against this library, and served to the ESDM:
.PP
.nf
cc \-o my\-provider my_provider.c $(pkg\-config \-\-cflags \-\-libs esdm_es_ext)
my\-provider \-\-socket /run/my\-es.socket
esdm\-server \-\-es_ext_socket /run/my\-es.socket
.fi
.PP
.I es\-ext/example/esdm_es_ext_example.c
of the source tree is such a program, reading a device \- non-blocking, with
a deadline per request \- with a self test, a status and a declared rate.
.PP
Two providers, served to an ESDM with two provider socket sources:
.PP
.nf
/usr/libexec/a\-provider \-\-socket /run/esdm\-es\-a.socket
/usr/libexec/b\-provider \-\-socket /run/esdm\-es\-b.socket
esdm\-server \-\-es_ext_socket /run/esdm\-es\-a.socket \\
	\-\-es_ext_socket /run/esdm\-es\-b.socket
.fi
.PP
A provider on the network, for the relay of the ESDM host 192.0.2.10:
.PP
.nf
my\-provider \-\-listen :@ES_EXT_NET_PORT@ \\
	\-\-cert /etc/esdm/es\-ext\-net/server.crt \\
	\-\-key /etc/esdm/es\-ext\-net/server.key \\
	\-\-ca /etc/esdm/es\-ext\-net/ca.crt \-\-allow 192.0.2.10
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR esdm\-server (8),
.BR esdm\-es\-ext\-relay (8),
.BR esdm\-es\-pkcs11 (8),
.BR esdm\-egd\-net\-server (8),
.BR libesdm (3)
